      "value": "So, the whole bitmasking thing is a bit too complex when we're talking about systems we want to expose to potentially amateur creators.\nInstead, we've introduced a new system wherein you have 128 bits of space for syncing. Floats and ints take 8, the newly-introduced bools take up 1 bit.\nThis allows you to do most of what your suggestion requests but with less complexity.\nThis is available in VRChat 2021.1.1, now in Open Beta. The patch notes are here: https://docs.vrchat.com/v2021.1.1/docs/latest-release#avatars-30\nRegardless of this _not entirely being the exact suggestion made_, I'm marking this as complete!",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2020-07-11T19:09:32.905Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "So we get 16 networked parameters, with each of them being (I assume) a 8-bit unsigned integer, with an option to map it to a floating point value for animator reasons. Avatars with toggleable elements (such as clothing or weapons) are extremely common with avatars 2.0, and avatars 3.0 brings some nice possibilities with it (Sync enabled objects! Perform different actions based on what's active! And more!), and while 16 synced parameters would be perfectly sufficient to support a fair amount of toggles as-is (well, 16 of them, if you don't want any other synced-parameter-driven things), that is certainly a sub-optimal use of a limited resource. At the same time, a single parameter could fit 8 independent binary toggles in it through the power of bit fiddling.\nMy suggestion, therefore, is to  add the following state behaviors:\n Set parameter bit\n  * Parameter name: any int parameter\n  * Bit index: which bit to set\n  * Bit value (toggle): value to set it to\nThis one is straightforward. It just changes parameter value. In code, it would be `param |= 1 << index` for set and `param &= ~(1 << index)` for unset.\nChange parameter if bit is set\n * Source bitmask: which parameter is tested\n * Source bit index: which bit to check\n * Target parameter: which parameter to change if the bit is set\n * Target value: the value it's changed to\nThis one would be used to untangle one bitfield parameter into multiple separate parameters. Right now, transition conditions don't have bit test as a comparison type, so with this behavior it would be possible to separate one (synced) bitfield into multiple (unsynced) booleans (or numeric parameters). In code, this would be `if((param & (1 << index)) != 0) target = value`.\nWithout this, properly handling a bitmask parameter would be hellishly hard, with full 8-depth binary tree of 256 states needing to be mapped out, or all 256 values to be handled manually. This allows a single state to drive 8 individual unsynced parameters on bit field change, and then 8 layers to handle 8 things toggled by 8 unsynced parameters.\nIn addition, a bit-based control (for setting/resetting bits from actions menu), or the ability to control unsynced parameters (so that they can be used to drive synced values from animation controller afterwards) would be very useful to make better use of these two behaviors.\nThis would allow creators to squeeze more out of current sync system, enabling avatars with more features and better (possibly) network usage.",
